Subject: [PATCH 09317/11362] Correct the k=8 margin claim: the tail is
 censored, so no concurrency guarantees it

The docstring claimed 8 is "the highest concurrency at which even the longest observed
induction fits". True of the longest COMPLETED induction, false in general, and the
distinction is the whole point: the length distribution is right-censored by the very
timeout being analysed. Three draws in the A/B corpus were cancelled at 3599 s and emit no
`eval time` line, so they are invisible to any distribution built from completed calls.
The largest reached 100,988 generated tokens, 98.6% of the cap, still climbing when the
client killed it. At k=8 that draw needs 2525 s against a 2400 s ceiling.

So 8 is a risk/throughput choice, not a guarantee, and no value can be a guarantee --
the mechanism that would record the tail is the mechanism that destroys it. The docstring
now says that, with the full table across the three reference lengths.

Also names the lever that would actually fix it, without taking it: the timeout and the
token cap were set independently, so the budget cannot be spent inside the window at ANY
concurrency including k=1. Making them consistent -- about 3277 s at k=8 -- covers the full
cap without giving up throughput. Operator decision.

Not reverting 24 -> 8. That change stands on the median result and is unaffected: at 24 the
median induction exceeded the timeout, and it still does.

